<Project>
  <!--
    AsmFoundation.Sdk - the consumer targets: imported into a .NET project that references an
    .asmproj (directly, or through another project), through the implicit AsmFoundation.Sdk
    PackageReference that every .asmproj carries (see the SDK's Properties.targets). NuGet
    imports them into the .asmproj itself too; everything here is off where
    UsingAsmFoundationSdk is true.

    Which builds a referenced asm project gets, the RID inferred by NuGet's own rules:
      RID-specific build of the .NET project (dotnet build/publish -r <rid>, or a
      RuntimeIdentifier in the project): each asm reference is built for the RID that serves
        this one best, by the .NET SDK's runtime identifier graph (the RID itself when the asm
        project builds it; linux-arm64 for linux-musl-arm64 when it builds only that one).
        ASM0040 when none of its RIDs fits; when the asm project sets
        OptionalOnUnlistedRuntimes=true, the warning ASM0049 instead, and this build goes on
        without it (its code must cope with the library's absence).
      NativeAOT publish (PublishAot, one RID): shared libraries and programs as above, and each
        static library is built for that RID and linked into the executable (DirectPInvoke and
        NativeLibrary items; see _AsmLinkStaticLibrariesIntoNativeAot).
      Portable build (no RuntimeIdentifier): each asm reference builds every RID it lists, or
        the RIDs of the ProjectReference's RuntimeIdentifiers metadata
        (<ProjectReference Include="..." RuntimeIdentifiers="linux-x64;win-x64" />).

    Where their libraries go (BinariesCopyPath), for build and publish alike:
      <ProjectReference Include="../Calc/Calc.asmproj" BinariesCopyPath="native/{rid}" />
      <PropertyGroup><BinariesCopyPath>RidFolders</BinariesCopyPath></PropertyGroup>
      Runtimes = runtimes/{rid}/native, AppFolder = next to the app, RidFolders = {rid},
      Auto (the default) = Runtimes in a portable build and AppFolder in a RID-specific one,
      which is what NuGet does with a package's native files; any other value is a folder
      inside the output, {rid} standing for the RID each file was built for. The value on
      the reference wins, then the property, then the explicit choice of a .NET library the
      asm library comes through (so BinariesCopyPath on a wrapper library's reference shapes
      the app's output too), then Auto. Rules and checks: the AsmResolveNativeLayouts and
      AsmLayOutNativeAssets tasks.
    How the app then finds them:
      deps.json lists each library with "localPath", the path it was copied to
        (AsmAddNativeAssetsToDepsFile): runtimeTargets in a portable app, a plain native asset
        in a RID-specific one. Measured with .NET 10: the host loads from any such folder,
        framework-dependent and self-contained alike.
      A C# project for .NET 5 or later gets a generated fallback loader (a
        ResolvingUnmanagedDll handler, AsmWriteNativeLibraryLoader) for what reads no
        deps.json (NativeAOT) or gets the files without an entry. AsmGenerateNativeLibraryLoader
        =false turns it off.

    Global properties that only make sense for the .NET app (self-contained, AOT, trimming,
    single file) are not passed on to the asm project: it builds native code either way, and
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk would act on them for its netstandard target framework. Neither is the
    app's output folder (dotnet build -o, -p:OutDir): the asm projects build into their own
    bin/ folders, and the app's folder gets the copies it needs.

    Four more things make the asm libraries behave like a package's native files:
      A .NET project delivers the files of its own asm references itself, already where its
        layout puts them (_AsmAddNativeCopyItems, _AsmAddNativePublishItems; the asm
        references are marked Private="false", so the common targets no longer ask the asm
        projects for their default places). What it hands a referencing project through
        GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems and GetCopyToPublishDirectoryItems is then laid out too.
        That matters for a referencing project that has none of these targets: one that gets a
        .NET library whose asm reference is PrivateAssets="all", which does not flow.
      A .NET class library between the app and the asm project (app -> library -> .asmproj)
        is RID-agnostic, so it is built portable even for a RID-specific app, and hands every
        RID's copy on to the app through GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems. The app lays out every
        asm file it gets, whichever way it came, and a RID-specific app keeps only its RID's
        (other RIDs' copies are dead files, the Windows and macOS ones in a Linux container
        image too, and NETSDK1236 in a single-file publish).
      dotnet publish marks the asm libraries AssetType=native, as NuGet marks a package's
        native files. The .NET SDK's single-file bundling decides by that metadata, which
        GetCopyToPublishDirectoryItems does not carry over: without it a libfoo.so or
        libfoo.dylib counted as unknown content (never bundled, even with
        IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract), and a foo.dll as an assembly the bundler then
        refused (NETSDK1236, and the file was neither bundled nor copied).
      dotnet pack of a .NET library puts an asm reference with PrivateAssets="all" INTO the
        package (runtimes/<rid>/native/, no dependency), with buildTransitive targets that give
        the package's consumers BinariesCopyPath too. Without PrivateAssets, the reference is a
        dependency on the asm project's own package, as NuGet makes any ProjectReference.

    Footgun: the asm files are recognized by path, not by metadata. The .NET SDK keeps only some
    metadata on the items GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems hands on (KeepMetadata), so the
    consumer asks the asm projects (GetAsmNativeRuntimeAssets) and the .NET libraries in between
    (GetAsmNativeAssets) which files are theirs, and matches the copy and publish lists against
    those full paths.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <_AsmConsumerTasksAssembly>$([MSBuild]::NormalizePath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)', '..', 'tasks', 'netstandard2.0', 'AsmFoundation.Sdk.Tasks.dll'))</_AsmConsumerTasksAssembly>
    <!-- Every RID AsmSdk builds: what a RID-less asm project can be built for. -->
    <_AsmConsumerSupportedRuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;win-arm64;linux-x64;linux-arm64;linux-musl-x64;linux-musl-arm64;osx-x64;osx-arm64</_AsmConsumerSupportedRuntimeIdentifiers>
    <!-- The app's publish switches, and its output folder: OutputPath and
         _CommandLineDefinedOutputPath from dotnet build -o, OutDir from -p:OutDir. A global
         output folder made every RID of a portable asm build write into the app's folder, one
         RID's static library overwriting another's ("machine type x64 conflicts with arm64",
         measured with dotnet build -r linux-x64 -o through a .NET class library). Also the
         consumer's own switches (BinariesCopyPath, AsmGenerateNativeLibraryLoader): an asm
         project builds the same files whatever a consumer does with them, and as a global
         property each value would make another instance of it, built again for nothing.
         PackAsTool too: a .NET tool packed with -p:PackAsTool=true would make each asm
         reference pack itself as a tool (and rename its RID builds' PackageId, the library
         name deps.json gives its files). -->

    <_AsmConsumerPropertiesToRemove>SelfContained;PublishAot;PublishTrimmed;PublishSingleFile;PublishReadyToRun;_IsPublishing;OutputPath;OutDir;_CommandLineDefinedOutputPath;BinariesCopyPath;AsmGenerateNativeLibraryLoader;PackAsTool</_AsmConsumerPropertiesToRemove>
    <!-- The generated fallback loader, in obj/. -->
    <_AsmNativeLibraryLoaderPath>$(IntermediateOutputPath)AsmNativeLibraryLoader.g.cs</_AsmNativeLibraryLoaderPath>
    <!-- Pack: asm references with PrivateAssets="all" go into this package. -->
    <TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage);_AsmEmbedNativeAssetsInPackage</TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <UsingTask TaskName="AsmFoundation.Sdk.Tasks.AsmSelectRuntimeIdentifier" AssemblyFile="$(_AsmConsumerTasksAssembly)" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'" />
  <UsingTask TaskName="AsmFoundation.Sdk.Tasks.AsmAddNativeAssetsToDepsFile" AssemblyFile="$(_AsmConsumerTasksAssembly)" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'" />
  <UsingTask TaskName="AsmFoundation.Sdk.Tasks.AsmResolveNativeLayouts" AssemblyFile="$(_AsmConsumerTasksAssembly)" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'" />
  <UsingTask TaskName="AsmFoundation.Sdk.Tasks.AsmLayOutNativeAssets" AssemblyFile="$(_AsmConsumerTasksAssembly)" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'" />
  <UsingTask TaskName="AsmFoundation.Sdk.Tasks.AsmWriteNativeLibraryLoader" AssemblyFile="$(_AsmConsumerTasksAssembly)" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'" />
  <UsingTask TaskName="AsmFoundation.Sdk.Tasks.AsmWritePackageConsumerTargets" AssemblyFile="$(_AsmConsumerTasksAssembly)" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'" />

  <!--
    _AsmConfigureAsmProjectReferences: runs right after the common targets have decided each
    reference's target framework (_GetProjectReferenceTargetFrameworkProperties), before any of
    them is built, and rewrites the .asmproj ones in _MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent, the list
    that ResolveProjectReferences, the copy-to-output step and the consumer targets below all
    call. Each reference is handled alone (target batching), because each asm project lists
    its own RIDs.
  -->
  <!-- DependsOnTargets, not CallTarget: a target called with CallTarget does not see the items
       its caller created, and the batching below needs them. -->
  <Target Name="_AsmConfigureAsmProjectReferences"
          AfterTargets="_GetProjectReferenceTargetFrameworkProperties"
          DependsOnTargets="_AsmFindAsmProjectReferences;_AsmConfigureEachAsmProjectReference"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'" />

  <Target Name="_AsmFindAsmProjectReferences">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmProjectReference Include="@(_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent)" Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.asmproj'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- One batch per asm reference. Conditioned on there being one: batching over an empty list
       still runs a target or task once (measured for item batching), with empty metadata. -->
  <Target Name="_AsmConfigureEachAsmProjectReference" Outputs="%(_AsmProjectReference.Identity)" Condition="'@(_AsmProjectReference)' != ''">
    <!-- Properties keep their values from one batch to the next: start each one empty. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
      <!-- The command word ASM0013 uses: pack when this .NET project is packed (an asm library
           embedded with PrivateAssets="all"), publish otherwise. -->
      <_AsmConsumerCommandWord Condition="'$(_IsPacking)' == 'true'">pack</_AsmConsumerCommandWord>
      <_AsmSelectedRid />
      <_AsmReferenceProperties />
      <_AsmReferenceKind />
      <_AsmReferenceRids />
      <_AsmReferenceImportName />
      <_AsmReferenceDelivered />
      <_AsmReferenceLinked />
      <_AsmReferenceUsed />
      <_AsmReferenceOptional />
      <_AsmReferenceOmitted />
      <_AsmReferenceProgramKind />
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- What the asm project produces and declares, asked without this project's RID (see
         GetAsmConsumerInfo); RIDs empty for one that declares none. -->
    <MSBuild Projects="@(_AsmProjectReference)"
             Targets="GetAsmConsumerInfo"
             Properties="%(_AsmProjectReference.SetConfiguration);%(_AsmProjectReference.SetPlatform);%(_AsmProjectReference.SetTargetFramework)"
             RemoveProperties="RuntimeIdentifier;$(_AsmConsumerPropertiesToRemove);%(_AsmProjectReference.GlobalPropertiesToRemove)">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_AsmReferenceInfo" />
    </MSBuild>
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_AsmReferenceKind>@(_AsmReferenceInfo->'%(AsmOutputKind)')</_AsmReferenceKind>
      <_AsmReferenceRids>@(_AsmReferenceInfo->'%(RuntimeIdentifiers)')</_AsmReferenceRids>
      <_AsmReferenceImportName>@(_AsmReferenceInfo->'%(AsmImportName)')</_AsmReferenceImportName>
      <_AsmReferenceOptional>@(_AsmReferenceInfo->'%(OptionalOnUnlistedRuntimes)')</_AsmReferenceOptional>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      A .NET app loads a shared library and starts a program (an Executable: the helper-process
      case, delivered with the libraries and programs it needs; see GetAsmNativeRuntimeAssets).
      A NativeAOT publish (PublishAot, one RID) also links a static library into the native
      executable (_AsmReferenceLinked; see _AsmLinkStaticLibrariesIntoNativeAot), as the
      interop guide used to have users wire by hand with DirectPInvoke and NativeLibrary items.
      Otherwise a static library or object files are left to the asm projects that link them:
      the .NET SDK adds an asm project's own asm references to this project as transitive
      ProjectReferences, and built from here, a static library that names no RID of its own
      would stop with ASM0010 (measured: a .NET app referencing a library that links a RID-less
      static library).
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_AsmReferenceDelivered Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceKind)' == 'SharedLibrary' or '$(_AsmReferenceKind)' == 'Executable'">true</_AsmReferenceDelivered>
      <_AsmReferenceLinked Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceKind)' == 'StaticLibrary' and '$(PublishAot)' == 'true' and '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">true</_AsmReferenceLinked>
      <_AsmReferenceUsed Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceDelivered)' == 'true' or '$(_AsmReferenceLinked)' == 'true'">true</_AsmReferenceUsed>
      <_AsmReferenceProgramKind Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceKind)' == 'Executable'">Program</_AsmReferenceProgramKind>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Message Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceUsed)' != 'true'"
             Importance="normal"
             Text="%(_AsmProjectReference.Filename)%(_AsmProjectReference.Extension) produces a $(_AsmReferenceKind), nothing a .NET app loads or starts (only a NativeAOT publish links a static library in): not built or copied through $(MSBuildProjectName)." />
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceUsed)' != 'true'">
      <_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent Remove="@(_AsmProjectReference)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
      ASM0013 for a portable publish or pack of this .NET project: the asm reference names no
      RIDs (neither in its project nor on this ProjectReference), so it is built for the build
      machine's RID alone, its default, and the published app or the package would carry only
      that platform's library. Warned here, in the .NET project, because this is where the
      command is known: _IsPublishing is one of the properties these targets keep from the
      asm project's build (see _AsmConsumerPropertiesToRemove), so that build and publish build
      it alike. A RID-specific publish asks for its own RID: no warning.
    -->
    <Warning Code="ASM0013"
             Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceDelivered)' == 'true' and '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == '' and '$(_AsmReferenceRids)' == '' and '%(_AsmProjectReference.RuntimeIdentifiers)' == '' and ('$(_IsPublishing)' == 'true' or '$(_IsPacking)' == 'true')"
             Text="%(_AsmProjectReference.Filename) lists no RuntimeIdentifiers, so this portable $([MSBuild]::ValueOrDefault('$(_AsmConsumerCommandWord)', 'publish')) of $(MSBuildProjectName) carries its library for the build machine's platform alone (its default). List the platforms in %(_AsmProjectReference.Filename)%(_AsmProjectReference.Extension) (&lt;RuntimeIdentifiers&gt;win-x64;linux-x64;osx-arm64&lt;/RuntimeIdentifiers&gt;) or on this ProjectReference (RuntimeIdentifiers=&quot;...&quot;), or publish for one platform with -r &lt;rid&gt;." />

    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceUsed)' == 'true' and '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">
      <_AsmReferenceDeclaredRid Include="$(_AsmReferenceRids)" />
      <_AsmReferenceDeclaredRid Include="$(_AsmConsumerSupportedRuntimeIdentifiers)" Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceRids)' == ''" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <!-- Optional: the asm project's OptionalOnUnlistedRuntimes. Then no serving RID is the
         warning ASM0049 and an empty selection, instead of the error ASM0040. -->
    <AsmSelectRuntimeIdentifier Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceUsed)' == 'true' and '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''"
                                RuntimeIdentifier="$(RuntimeIdentifier)"
                                AvailableRuntimeIdentifiers="@(_AsmReferenceDeclaredRid)"
                                RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath="$(RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath)"
                                ProjectName="%(_AsmProjectReference.Filename)%(_AsmProjectReference.Extension)"
                                Optional="$([MSBuild]::ValueOrDefault('$(_AsmReferenceOptional)', 'false'))">
      <Output TaskParameter="Selected" PropertyName="_AsmSelectedRid" />
    </AsmSelectRuntimeIdentifier>

    <!-- An optional asm project without a build for this RID: this build goes on without it,
         as if there were no reference (nothing built, copied, listed in deps.json or linked).
         The app's code that calls it gets DllNotFoundException, which it said it handles. -->
    <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceUsed)' == 'true' and '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != '' and '$(_AsmSelectedRid)' == ''">
      <_AsmReferenceOmitted>true</_AsmReferenceOmitted>
      <_AsmReferenceUsed />
      <_AsmReferenceDelivered />
      <_AsmReferenceLinked />
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceOmitted)' == 'true'">
      <_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent Remove="@(_AsmProjectReference)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <PropertyGroup>
      <!-- RID-specific: build the selected RID (the .NET project's own RID would flow there
           otherwise, asm projects not being RID-agnostic). Portable: the RIDs the
           ProjectReference asks for, with commas (see AsmRequestedRuntimeIdentifiers). -->
      <_AsmReferenceProperties Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">RuntimeIdentifier=$(_AsmSelectedRid)</_AsmReferenceProperties>
      <_AsmReferenceProperties Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == '' and '%(_AsmProjectReference.RuntimeIdentifiers)' != ''">AsmRequestedRuntimeIdentifiers=$([System.String]::Copy('%(_AsmProjectReference.RuntimeIdentifiers)').Replace(';', ',').Replace(' ', ''))</_AsmReferenceProperties>
      <_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties>%(_AsmProjectReference.AdditionalProperties)</_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties>
      <_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceProperties)' != '' and '$(_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties)' != ''">$(_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties);$(_AsmReferenceProperties)</_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties>
      <_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceProperties)' != '' and '%(_AsmProjectReference.AdditionalProperties)' == ''">$(_AsmReferenceProperties)</_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties>
      <_AsmReferenceGlobalPropertiesToRemove>%(_AsmProjectReference.GlobalPropertiesToRemove);$(_AsmConsumerPropertiesToRemove)</_AsmReferenceGlobalPropertiesToRemove>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Replaced, not modified: the item goes back with the new metadata (at the end of the
         list, which changes nothing but the order references are visited in). The library is
         also recorded for BinariesCopyPath (_AsmResolveNativeLayouts): its project, its name,
         and the value on THIS project's reference to it (empty on a transitive reference).
         Private="false": the common GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems and
         GetCopyToPublishDirectoryItems skip such a reference, so the asm project's copies at
         their default places never enter this project's lists; this project delivers them
         itself, laid out (_AsmComputeDeliveredNativeAssets). Safe because Private decides
         nothing else for a reference that yields no assembly (GetTargetPathWithTargetPlatformMoniker
         returns none). A Private="false" the project wrote itself is kept as AsmCopyLocal and
         still means "no copies". -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceUsed)' == 'true'">
      <_AsmConfiguredReference Include="@(_AsmProjectReference)">
        <AdditionalProperties>$(_AsmReferenceAdditionalProperties)</AdditionalProperties>
        <GlobalPropertiesToRemove>$(_AsmReferenceGlobalPropertiesToRemove)</GlobalPropertiesToRemove>
        <AsmCopyLocal>%(_AsmProjectReference.Private)</AsmCopyLocal>
        <Private>false</Private>
      </_AsmConfiguredReference>
      <_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent Remove="@(_AsmProjectReference)" />
      <_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent Include="@(_AsmConfiguredReference)" />
      <!-- AsmKind Program keeps a program out of the generated loader's table: it is started,
           never loaded through [DllImport]. A static library linked into NativeAOT is neither
           copied nor listed: its code is inside the executable. -->
      <_AsmNativeLibrary Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceDelivered)' == 'true'" Include="%(_AsmProjectReference.FullPath)" AsmImportName="$(_AsmReferenceImportName)" BinariesCopyPath="%(_AsmProjectReference.BinariesCopyPath)" AsmKind="$([MSBuild]::ValueOrDefault('$(_AsmReferenceProgramKind)', 'Library'))" />
      <!-- NativeAOT: the configured reference (with its RID and properties, so that the call
           for its link inputs reaches the very build ResolveProjectReferences ran), and
           DirectPInvoke for its name: ILC then calls the library's functions directly, and the
           linker resolves them in the archive, instead of a dlopen at run time. The name is
           the library's AssemblyName, the one [LibraryImport] and [DllImport] use. -->
      <_AsmAotStaticLibrary Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceLinked)' == 'true'" Include="@(_AsmConfiguredReference)" AsmImportName="$(_AsmReferenceImportName)" />
      <DirectPInvoke Condition="'$(_AsmReferenceLinked)' == 'true'" Include="$(_AsmReferenceImportName)" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmConfiguredReference Remove="@(_AsmConfiguredReference)" />
      <_AsmReferenceDeclaredRid Remove="@(_AsmReferenceDeclaredRid)" />
      <_AsmReferenceInfo Remove="@(_AsmReferenceInfo)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmLinkStaticLibrariesIntoNativeAot: a NativeAOT publish links each static library this
    project references (see _AsmReferenceLinked above) into its native executable, as
    NativeLibrary items, beside the DirectPInvoke items already added for their names. The
    paths come from the static library's own GetAsmLinkInputs (its LinkInput, the .lib or .a
    of the selected RID), asked of the same build ResolveProjectReferences just ran: the same
    global properties, so MSBuild reuses that project instance and nothing builds twice.
    NativeAOT reads DirectPInvoke when it writes the compiler's response file and NativeLibrary
    when it links (LinkNative), both after Build, so adding them here is in time; a
    dotnet build of a PublishAot project gets them too, unused. A static library a static
    library references comes along as a transitive ProjectReference, so it is linked as well.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmLinkStaticLibrariesIntoNativeAot"
          AfterTargets="ResolveProjectReferences"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true' and '@(_AsmAotStaticLibrary)' != ''">
    <MSBuild Projects="@(_AsmAotStaticLibrary)"
             Targets="GetAsmLinkInputs"
             BuildInParallel="$(BuildInParallel)"
             Properties="%(_AsmAotStaticLibrary.SetConfiguration);%(_AsmAotStaticLibrary.SetPlatform);%(_AsmAotStaticLibrary.SetTargetFramework)"
             RemoveProperties="%(_AsmAotStaticLibrary.GlobalPropertiesToRemove)$(_GlobalPropertiesToRemoveFromProjectReferences)">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_AsmAotLinkInput" />
    </MSBuild>
    <ItemGroup>
      <!-- LinkInput only: the include directories GetAsmLinkInputs also returns are for asm
           projects that assemble against the library. -->
      <NativeLibrary Include="@(_AsmAotLinkInput)" Condition="'%(_AsmAotLinkInput.AsmItemType)' == 'LinkInput'" />
      <_AsmAotLinkInput Remove="@(_AsmAotLinkInput)" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Message Importance="normal" Text="NativeAOT links @(_AsmAotStaticLibrary->'%(Filename)', ', ') into $(MSBuildProjectName) (DirectPInvoke @(_AsmAotStaticLibrary->'%(AsmImportName)', ', '))." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmResolveNativeLayouts: where each asm library this project deploys goes
    (BinariesCopyPath), decided once per build: the libraries of its own asm references (from
    _AsmConfigureEachAsmProjectReference) and those its .NET references report
    (GetAsmNativeLibraries, which only a project with these targets has, hence
    SkipNonexistentTargets). Needs no build output, so it can run before CoreCompile, for the
    loader.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmResolveNativeLayouts" DependsOnTargets="PrepareProjectReferences" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmDotnetProjectReference Include="@(_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent)" Condition="'%(Extension)' != '.asmproj'" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <MSBuild Condition="'@(_AsmDotnetProjectReference)' != ''"
             Projects="@(_AsmDotnetProjectReference)"
             Targets="GetAsmNativeLibraries"
             BuildInParallel="$(BuildInParallel)"
             SkipNonexistentTargets="true"
             Properties="%(_AsmDotnetProjectReference.SetConfiguration);%(_AsmDotnetProjectReference.SetPlatform);%(_AsmDotnetProjectReference.SetTargetFramework)"
             RemoveProperties="%(_AsmDotnetProjectReference.GlobalPropertiesToRemove)$(_GlobalPropertiesToRemoveFromProjectReferences)">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_AsmNativeLibrary" />
    </MSBuild>
    <AsmResolveNativeLayouts Condition="'@(_AsmNativeLibrary)' != '' or '$(BinariesCopyPath)' != ''"
                             Libraries="@(_AsmNativeLibrary)"
                             ProjectBinariesCopyPath="$(BinariesCopyPath)"
                             RuntimeIdentifier="$(RuntimeIdentifier)"
                             ProjectFile="$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)">
      <Output TaskParameter="Layouts" ItemName="_AsmNativeLayout" />
    </AsmResolveNativeLayouts>
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmDotnetProjectReference Remove="@(_AsmDotnetProjectReference)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    GetAsmNativeLibraries: what this project tells a project that references it, so that the
    referencing project can lay out the same libraries (and, when it says nothing itself, the
    way this one chose): each library with AsmImportName, ReportedExplicit (whether this
    project chose its layout itself), ReportedBinariesCopyPath (that choice, normalized) and
    ReportedBy.
  -->
  <Target Name="GetAsmNativeLibraries" DependsOnTargets="_AsmResolveNativeLayouts" Returns="@(_AsmNativeLibraryReport)" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmNativeLibraryReport Include="@(_AsmNativeLayout)">
        <ReportedExplicit>%(_AsmNativeLayout.Explicit)</ReportedExplicit>
        <ReportedBinariesCopyPath Condition="'%(_AsmNativeLayout.Explicit)' == 'true'">%(_AsmNativeLayout.BinariesCopyPath)</ReportedBinariesCopyPath>
        <ReportedBy>$(MSBuildProjectName)</ReportedBy>
        <BinariesCopyPath />
      </_AsmNativeLibraryReport>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmCollectKnownNativeAssets: every asm file this project may receive, after the
    references are built (paths of finished builds, PDBs included): its asm references' own
    (GetAsmNativeRuntimeAssets, from the same builds the references already ran) and what its
    .NET references know (GetAsmNativeAssets, recursively), each with RuntimeIdentifier,
    AsmProject, AsmLibraryName, AsmLibraryVersion, AsmImportName and AsmSymbols.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmCollectKnownNativeAssets" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets Include="@(_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent)" Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.asmproj'" />
      <_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets Include="@(_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent)" Condition="'%(Extension)' != '.asmproj'" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <MSBuild Condition="'@(_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets)' != ''"
             Projects="@(_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets)"
             Targets="GetAsmNativeRuntimeAssets"
             BuildInParallel="$(BuildInParallel)"
             Properties="%(_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets.SetConfiguration);%(_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets.SetPlatform);%(_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets.SetTargetFramework)"
             RemoveProperties="%(_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets.GlobalPropertiesToRemove)$(_GlobalPropertiesToRemoveFromProjectReferences)">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_AsmKnownNativeAsset" />
    </MSBuild>
    <MSBuild Condition="'@(_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets)' != ''"
             Projects="@(_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets)"
             Targets="GetAsmNativeAssets"
             BuildInParallel="$(BuildInParallel)"
             SkipNonexistentTargets="true"
             Properties="%(_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets.SetConfiguration);%(_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets.SetPlatform);%(_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets.SetTargetFramework)"
             RemoveProperties="%(_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets.GlobalPropertiesToRemove)$(_GlobalPropertiesToRemoveFromProjectReferences)">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_AsmKnownNativeAsset" />
    </MSBuild>
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets Remove="@(_AsmProjectReferenceForAssets)" />
      <_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets Remove="@(_AsmDotnetReferenceForAssets)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- GetAsmNativeAssets: the asm files this project knows, for a project that references it. -->
  <Target Name="GetAsmNativeAssets" DependsOnTargets="_AsmCollectKnownNativeAssets" Returns="@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'" />

  <!--
    _AsmComputeDeployedNativeAssets: the files this project deploys and where (RelativePath),
    the same rule the copy and publish steps apply, for deps.json.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmComputeDeployedNativeAssets" DependsOnTargets="_AsmResolveNativeLayouts;_AsmCollectKnownNativeAssets" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <AsmLayOutNativeAssets Condition="'@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)' != ''"
                           Assets="@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)"
                           Layouts="@(_AsmNativeLayout)"
                           RuntimeIdentifier="$(RuntimeIdentifier)"
                           RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath="$(RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath)"
                           DestinationMetadata="RelativePath"
                           ProjectFile="$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)">
      <Output TaskParameter="LaidOut" ItemName="_AsmDeployedNativeAsset" />
    </AsmLayOutNativeAssets>
    <ItemGroup>
      <!-- deps.json lists libraries, not their PDBs. -->
      <_AsmNativeAssetForDeps Include="@(_AsmDeployedNativeAsset)" Condition="'%(_AsmDeployedNativeAsset.AsmSymbols)' != 'true'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- The build's deps.json (bin/), for dotnet run, dotnet test and the build output itself. -->
  <Target Name="_AsmAddNativeAssetsToBuildDepsFile"
          AfterTargets="GenerateBuildDependencyFile"
          DependsOnTargets="_AsmComputeDeployedNativeAssets"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true' and '$(GenerateDependencyFile)' == 'true' and Exists('$(ProjectDepsFilePath)')">
    <AsmAddNativeAssetsToDepsFile Condition="'@(_AsmNativeAssetForDeps)' != ''"
                                  DepsFilePath="$(ProjectDepsFilePath)"
                                  NativeAssets="@(_AsmNativeAssetForDeps)"
                                  MainAssemblyFileName="$(TargetFileName)" />
  </Target>

  <!-- The publish deps.json, when publish writes its own instead of copying the build's. -->
  <Target Name="_AsmAddNativeAssetsToPublishDepsFile"
          AfterTargets="GeneratePublishDependencyFile"
          DependsOnTargets="_AsmComputeDeployedNativeAssets"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true' and '$(GenerateDependencyFile)' == 'true' and Exists('$(PublishDepsFilePath)')">
    <AsmAddNativeAssetsToDepsFile Condition="'@(_AsmNativeAssetForDeps)' != ''"
                                  DepsFilePath="$(PublishDepsFilePath)"
                                  NativeAssets="@(_AsmNativeAssetForDeps)"
                                  MainAssemblyFileName="$(TargetFileName)" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmComputeDeliveredNativeAssets: the files of this project's own asm references (not those
    a .NET reference hands on), each with TargetPath where this project's layout puts it, and
    in a RID-specific build only its RID's. This project delivers them itself through its copy
    and publish lists (the two targets below), the common targets no longer asking the asm
    projects (Private="false", see _AsmConfigureEachAsmProjectReference).
    Why, measured: GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems fixes what it hands a referencing project when
    it finishes (it collects the references' items with CallTarget, whose outputs are taken
    when the called target ends), before any AfterTargets of its own run. A referencing
    project without these targets copies exactly what it is handed: an app that gets a .NET
    library whose asm reference is PrivateAssets="all" (which does not flow, nor do these
    targets) received the asm project's runtimes/<rid>/native/ places while the library's
    loader looked in native/<rid>/, and failed with DllNotFoundException on win-x64,
    linux-x64, linux-musl-x64 and linux-arm64. Delivered laid out from the start, the files
    are where this project put them, in its own output and in every output that copies from it.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmComputeDeliveredNativeAssets" DependsOnTargets="_AsmResolveNativeLayouts" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmDeliveredReference Include="@(_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent)" Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.asmproj' and '%(_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent.AsmCopyLocal)' != 'false'" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <!-- The same builds the references already ran (same global properties), so MSBuild hands
         back results it has; no asm project is evaluated or built again. -->
    <MSBuild Condition="'@(_AsmDeliveredReference)' != ''"
             Projects="@(_AsmDeliveredReference)"
             Targets="GetAsmNativeRuntimeAssets"
             BuildInParallel="$(BuildInParallel)"
             Properties="%(_AsmDeliveredReference.SetConfiguration);%(_AsmDeliveredReference.SetPlatform);%(_AsmDeliveredReference.SetTargetFramework)"
             RemoveProperties="%(_AsmDeliveredReference.GlobalPropertiesToRemove)$(_GlobalPropertiesToRemoveFromProjectReferences)">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_AsmDeliveredAsset" />
    </MSBuild>
    <AsmLayOutNativeAssets Condition="'@(_AsmDeliveredAsset)' != ''"
                           Assets="@(_AsmDeliveredAsset)"
                           Layouts="@(_AsmNativeLayout)"
                           RuntimeIdentifier="$(RuntimeIdentifier)"
                           RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath="$(RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath)"
                           DestinationMetadata="TargetPath"
                           ProjectFile="$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)">
      <Output TaskParameter="LaidOut" ItemName="_AsmDeliveredNativeFile" />
    </AsmLayOutNativeAssets>
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmDeliveredReference Remove="@(_AsmDeliveredReference)" />
      <_AsmDeliveredAsset Remove="@(_AsmDeliveredAsset)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmAddNativeCopyItems: the delivered files into the build's copy list. BeforeTargets runs
    after GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems' DependsOnTargets and before its body, which appends
    to _SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectory and then returns AllItemsFullPathWithTargetPath
    built from it: the files are copied here and handed on with this TargetPath.
    PreserveNewest, as the asm project's own items were.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmAddNativeCopyItems"
          BeforeTargets="GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems"
          DependsOnTargets="_AsmComputeDeliveredNativeAssets"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectory Include="@(_AsmDeliveredNativeFile)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmAddNativePublishItems: the same for dotnet publish, through GetCopyToPublishDirectoryItems
    (one target: its child calls and its AllPublishItemsFullPathWithTargetPath both come after
    BeforeTargets). ExcludeFromSingleFile="true", one of the three metadata that survive the hand-on
    (CopyToPublishDirectory, ExcludeFromSingleFile, TargetPath): a project without these targets
    cannot mark the files AssetType=native, and the .NET SDK's single-file step then counts a
    foo.dll as an assembly the bundler refuses (NETSDK1236: neither bundled nor copied). Excluded,
    it stays beside the bundle, where the library's loader looks. A project WITH these targets
    clears it again in _AsmLayOutNativePublishFiles, where AssetType=native decides.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmAddNativePublishItems"
          BeforeTargets="GetCopyToPublishDirectoryItems"
          DependsOnTargets="_AsmComputeDeliveredNativeAssets"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_SourceItemsToCopyToPublishDirectory Include="@(_AsmDeliveredNativeFile)" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" ExcludeFromSingleFile="true" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmLayOutNativeCopies: the build's copy lists with every asm file where BinariesCopyPath
    puts it, each file once, and in a RID-specific build only its RID's. After
    GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems, which fills the lists that the copy step
    (_CopyOutOfDateSourceItemsToOutputDirectory and its Always and IfDifferent variants) reads
    next. The asm items are found by full path (Exclude matching), laid out by the task, and
    put back into the list their CopyToOutputDirectory names.
    What it changes is this project's own output only: what GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems hands
    a referencing project was fixed before this target runs. That is why the files of this
    project's own asm references arrive laid out already (_AsmAddNativeCopyItems); the files
    that came through .NET references are laid out again here, and a referencing project that
    has these targets lays out whatever it gets itself (it learns this project's choices from
    GetAsmNativeLibraries).
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmLayOutNativeCopies"
          AfterTargets="GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems"
          DependsOnTargets="_AsmResolveNativeLayouts;_AsmCollectKnownNativeAssets"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup Condition="'@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)' != ''">
      <_AsmCopyCandidate Include="@(_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectory);@(_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectoryAlways);@(_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectoryIfDifferent)" />
      <_AsmCopyOther Include="@(_AsmCopyCandidate)" Exclude="@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)" />
      <_AsmCopyNative Include="@(_AsmCopyCandidate)" Exclude="@(_AsmCopyOther)" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <AsmLayOutNativeAssets Condition="'@(_AsmCopyNative)' != ''"
                           Assets="@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)"
                           Layouts="@(_AsmNativeLayout)"
                           RuntimeIdentifier="$(RuntimeIdentifier)"
                           RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath="$(RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath)"
                           DestinationMetadata="TargetPath"
                           ListItems="@(_AsmCopyNative)"
                           ProjectFile="$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)">
      <Output TaskParameter="LaidOut" ItemName="_AsmLaidOutCopy" />
    </AsmLayOutNativeAssets>
    <ItemGroup Condition="'@(_AsmCopyNative)' != ''">
      <_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectory Remove="@(_AsmCopyNative)" />
      <_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectoryAlways Remove="@(_AsmCopyNative)" />
      <_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectoryIfDifferent Remove="@(_AsmCopyNative)" />
      <AllItemsFullPathWithTargetPath Remove="@(_AsmCopyNative)" />
      <_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectoryAlways Include="@(_AsmLaidOutCopy)" Condition="'%(_AsmLaidOutCopy.CopyToOutputDirectory)' == 'Always'" />
      <_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectoryIfDifferent Include="@(_AsmLaidOutCopy)" Condition="'%(_AsmLaidOutCopy.CopyToOutputDirectory)' == 'IfDifferent'" />
      <_SourceItemsToCopyToOutputDirectory Include="@(_AsmLaidOutCopy)" Condition="'%(_AsmLaidOutCopy.CopyToOutputDirectory)' != 'Always' and '%(_AsmLaidOutCopy.CopyToOutputDirectory)' != 'IfDifferent'" />
      <AllItemsFullPathWithTargetPath Include="@(_AsmLaidOutCopy)" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmCopyCandidate Remove="@(_AsmCopyCandidate)" />
      <_AsmCopyOther Remove="@(_AsmCopyOther)" />
      <_AsmCopyNative Remove="@(_AsmCopyNative)" />
      <_AsmLaidOutCopy Remove="@(_AsmLaidOutCopy)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmLayOutNativePublishFiles: the publish list as NuGet would make it for a package with
    native files, before anything reads it (single-file bundling, the copy, containers): each
    asm file where BinariesCopyPath puts it (RelativePath), only the RID's in a RID-specific
    publish, and each library (not its PDB) AssetType=native, the metadata the .NET SDK's
    single-file step classifies by (_ComputeFilesToBundle). Measured with the .NET 11 SDK
    before AssetType was set: -p:PublishSingleFile=true on win-x64 published a program whose
    qamath.dll was neither in the bundle nor beside it (NETSDK1236, and DllNotFoundException
    at run time), and on Linux IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract left libqamath.so outside
    the bundle. Now a native library stays beside the bundle, as a NuGet package's does,
    unless IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract puts it inside.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmLayOutNativePublishFiles"
          AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList"
          DependsOnTargets="_AsmResolveNativeLayouts;_AsmCollectKnownNativeAssets"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup Condition="'@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)' != ''">
      <_AsmPublishOther Include="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)" Exclude="@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)" />
      <_AsmPublishNative Include="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)" Exclude="@(_AsmPublishOther)" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <AsmLayOutNativeAssets Condition="'@(_AsmPublishNative)' != ''"
                           Assets="@(_AsmKnownNativeAsset)"
                           Layouts="@(_AsmNativeLayout)"
                           RuntimeIdentifier="$(RuntimeIdentifier)"
                           RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath="$(RuntimeIdentifierGraphPath)"
                           DestinationMetadata="RelativePath"
                           ListItems="@(_AsmPublishNative)"
                           ProjectFile="$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)">
      <Output TaskParameter="LaidOut" ItemName="_AsmLaidOutPublish" />
    </AsmLayOutNativeAssets>
    <!-- ExcludeFromSingleFile="" undoes the "true" that _AsmAddNativePublishItems sets for
         projects without these targets: here AssetType=native decides, so that a library stays
         beside the bundle unless IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract puts it inside, as for a
         package's native files. -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'@(_AsmPublishNative)' != ''">
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Remove="@(_AsmPublishNative)" />
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Include="@(_AsmLaidOutPublish)" Condition="'%(_AsmLaidOutPublish.AsmSymbols)' == 'true'" ExcludeFromSingleFile="" />
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Include="@(_AsmLaidOutPublish)" Condition="'%(_AsmLaidOutPublish.AsmSymbols)' != 'true'" AssetType="native" ExcludeFromSingleFile="" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmPublishOther Remove="@(_AsmPublishOther)" />
      <_AsmPublishNative Remove="@(_AsmPublishNative)" />
      <_AsmLaidOutPublish Remove="@(_AsmLaidOutPublish)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmGenerateNativeLibraryLoader: the fallback loader of a C# project for .NET 5 or later
    that deploys an asm library anywhere but the app folder (a portable build's default,
    runtimes/{rid}/native, counts). Written only when its text changes; nothing for a project
    whose libraries all sit next to the app.
    Before BeforeCompile, not just before CoreCompile: _GenerateCompileDependencyCache hashes
    @(Compile) in between, and that hash is what makes CoreCompile run again when a file
    leaves the list. Added later, a loader that a changed layout no longer needs left the old
    assembly, with the loader, in place (measured: CoreCompile was skipped as up to date).
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmGenerateNativeLibraryLoader"
          BeforeTargets="BeforeCompile"
          DependsOnTargets="_AsmResolveNativeLayouts"
          Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true' and '$(Language)' == 'C#' and '$(AsmGenerateNativeLibraryLoader)' != 'false' and '$(TargetFrameworkIdentifier)' == '.NETCoreApp' and $([MSBuild]::VersionGreaterThanOrEquals('$(TargetFrameworkVersion.TrimStart(`vV`))', '5.0'))">
    <AsmWriteNativeLibraryLoader Condition="'@(_AsmNativeLayout)' != ''"
                                 Layouts="@(_AsmNativeLayout)"
                                 OutputPath="$(_AsmNativeLibraryLoaderPath)"
                                 Origin="AsmFoundation.Sdk for $(MSBuildProjectName)">
      <Output TaskParameter="Written" PropertyName="_AsmNativeLibraryLoaderWritten" />
    </AsmWriteNativeLibraryLoader>
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_AsmNativeLibraryLoaderWritten)' == 'true'">
      <Compile Include="$(_AsmNativeLibraryLoaderPath)" />
      <FileWrites Include="$(_AsmNativeLibraryLoaderPath)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    _AsmEmbedNativeAssetsInPackage (dotnet pack of a .NET library, per target framework):
    every RID's library of each asm reference that has PrivateAssets="all" goes into the
    package under runtimes/<rid>/native/, where NuGet gives each consumer its RID's (no PDBs, as
    a package's native files usually go without). NuGet already leaves such a reference out of
    the dependencies. The package also gets buildTransitive/<tfm>/<id>.targets, so its consumers
    can move those files with BinariesCopyPath like an asm package's.
    An asm program (the helper-process case) goes in the same way, with the libraries and
    programs it needs, at their paths relative to it (AsmBundlePath), and stays out of the
    loader's table. Those paths must be flat (ASM0051): NuGet copies a package's runtimes/
    files flat into a consumer built for one RID (measured with the .NET 11 SDK:
    runtimes/linux-x64/native/lib/linux-x64/libx.so arrived as ./libx.so), so a program whose
    run path names a subfolder would not find its libraries there.
  -->
  <Target Name="_AsmEmbedNativeAssetsInPackage" DependsOnTargets="PrepareProjectReferences" Condition="'$(UsingAsmFoundationSdk)' != 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmEmbeddedReference Include="@(_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent)"
                             Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.asmproj' and $([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch('%(_MSBuildProjectReferenceExistent.PrivateAssets)', '(?i)(^|;)\s*all\s*(;|$)'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <MSBuild Condition="'@(_AsmEmbeddedReference)' != ''"
             Projects="@(_AsmEmbeddedReference)"
             Targets="GetAsmNativeRuntimeAssets"
             BuildInParallel="$(BuildInParallel)"
             Properties="%(_AsmEmbeddedReference.SetConfiguration);%(_AsmEmbeddedReference.SetPlatform);%(_AsmEmbeddedReference.SetTargetFramework)"
             RemoveProperties="%(_AsmEmbeddedReference.GlobalPropertiesToRemove)$(_GlobalPropertiesToRemoveFromProjectReferences)">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_AsmEmbeddedAsset" />
    </MSBuild>
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AsmEmbeddedLibrary Include="@(_AsmEmbeddedAsset)" Condition="'%(_AsmEmbeddedAsset.AsmSymbols)' != 'true'">
        <!-- Where it goes under runtimes/<rid>/native/: a library flat, a program's file at its
             path relative to the program. -->
        <AsmPackageFile>%(Filename)%(Extension)</AsmPackageFile>
        <AsmPackageFile Condition="'%(_AsmEmbeddedAsset.AsmBundlePath)' != ''">%(_AsmEmbeddedAsset.AsmBundlePath)</AsmPackageFile>
      </_AsmEmbeddedLibrary>
      <_AsmEmbeddedName Include="@(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary->'%(AsmImportName)')" Condition="'%(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary.AsmKind)' != 'Program'" />
      <_AsmEmbeddedNested Include="@(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary)" Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary.AsmPackageFile)').Contains('/'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error Code="ASM0051"
           Condition="'@(_AsmEmbeddedNested)' != ''"
           Text="An asm program goes into a package with the libraries and programs it needs next to it, and these are in subfolders: @(_AsmEmbeddedNested->'%(AsmPackageFile)', ', '). NuGet copies a package's runtimes/ files flat into a consumer built for one RID, where the program's run path would then find nothing. Keep them next to the program: BinariesCopyPath=&quot;AppFolder&quot; (the default) on the program's references." />
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_AsmEmbeddedTargetsPath>$(IntermediateOutputPath)asmsdk-pack/$(PackageId).targets</_AsmEmbeddedTargetsPath>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <AsmWritePackageConsumerTargets Condition="'@(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary)' != ''"
                                    PackageId="$(PackageId)"
                                    PackageVersion="$(PackageVersion)"
                                    Origin="AsmFoundation.Sdk"
                                    LibraryNames="@(_AsmEmbeddedName)"
                                    OutputPath="$(_AsmEmbeddedTargetsPath)" />
    <ItemGroup Condition="'@(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary)' != ''">
      <!-- A program's files get their folder as PackagePath: NuGet reads a PackagePath without
           the source's extension as a folder and appends the file name, so an extensionless
           Linux or macOS program would come out as .../native/hello/hello (measured). Its bundle
           is flat (ASM0051), so each file keeps its own name there. -->
      <TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="@(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary)" Condition="'%(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary.AsmKind)' != 'Program'" PackagePath="runtimes/%(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary.RuntimeIdentifier)/native/%(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary.AsmPackageFile)" />
      <TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="@(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary)" Condition="'%(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary.AsmKind)' == 'Program'" PackagePath="runtimes/%(_AsmEmbeddedLibrary.RuntimeIdentifier)/native/" />
      <TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="$(_AsmEmbeddedTargetsPath)" PackagePath="buildTransitive/$(TargetFramework)/$(PackageId).targets" />
      <FileWrites Include="$(_AsmEmbeddedTargetsPath)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>
</Project>
